VANGERS: One for the Road
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

1. Required libraries:

   - SDL 3.2 or newer
   - SDL_net 3.2 or newer
   - libvorbis
   - SDL3-native clunk from the upstream `master` branch
     (https://github.com/stalkerg/clunk)
   - ffmpeg 6.0 or newer
   - toml11 4.4.x
   - zlib

2. Required tools:

   - CMake 4.0 or newer
   - Ninja or make

3. Building:

   $ mkdir build
   $ cd build
   $ cmake ..
   $ make

4. Running:

   $ cd data
   $ ../build/src/vangers


Windows (MSYS2) helper scripts

The OSS Windows build can be reproduced locally with the scripts in
packaging/msys2. They mirror the "OSS Windows 64bit Build" GitHub Actions job
and are split into three steps:

   > powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File packaging\msys2\install-msys2.ps1
   > powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File packaging\msys2\build-msys2.ps1
   > powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File packaging\msys2\run-msys2.ps1 <game-dir>

install-msys2.ps1 installs MSYS2 automatically (into C:\msys64 by default) when
it is not present, updates it, and installs the required packages plus the pinned
clunk and toml11; use -Msys2Root, -NoInstallMsys2, -ClunkRepo, -ClunkCommit,
-Msys2Version, -Msys2InstallerUrl, -Msys2Sha256 and -NoUpdate to override the
defaults. build-msys2.ps1 only configures, builds and tests Vangers (use
-BuildType, -BuildDir and -SkipTests). run-msys2.ps1 copies the built binaries and
their MSYS2 runtime DLLs into <game-dir> and launches the game (add -NoRun to copy
only).

The underlying .sh scripts can also be run directly from an MSYS2 UCRT64 shell.
install-msys2.sh requires a completed full update: either run it as
`MSYS2_UPDATED=1 ./packaging/msys2/install-msys2.sh` after updating manually
(pacman -Syu, reopening the terminal if it updated msys2-runtime, then pacman -Syu
again), or use the PowerShell entry point, which drives the restart.


Windows (MSVC) prerequisites

a. Install Visual Studio 2022 or newer with the "Desktop development with C++"
   workload (CMake and Ninja are included with the workload).
b. Install vcpkg and set VCPKG_ROOT to its directory:
   https://learn.microsoft.com/vcpkg/get_started/get-started
   Dependencies are declared in vcpkg.json (sdl3, sdl3-net, libogg, libvorbis,
   zlib, toml11). Install them from the Vangers root into vcpkg_installed:

   > vcpkg install --triplet x64-windows
c. Obtain an MSVC build of FFmpeg (6.0 or newer). Prebuilt shared packages
   with headers and import libraries are available from
   https://github.com/System233/ffmpeg-msvc-prebuilt (x64, LGPL). Extract the
   archive and pass the prefix as -DFFMPEG_ROOT=<ffmpeg-prefix>. The helper
   script below downloads it into external\ffmpeg automatically.
d. Build and install clunk with the MSVC toolchain from the upstream `master`
   branch (https://github.com/stalkerg/clunk). clunk has no vcpkg manifest of
   its own, so point it at the dependencies installed in step b; pass its
   install prefix to Vangers as -DCLUNK_ROOT=<clunk-prefix>:

   > git clone https://github.com/stalkerg/clunk.git external/clunk
   > cmake -S external/clunk -B external/clunk/build -G Ninja ^
       -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release ^
       -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=%CD%\vcpkg_installed\x64-windows ^
       -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=%CD%\external\clunk-install
   > cmake --build external/clunk/build --parallel
   > cmake --install external/clunk/build
e. Configure and build Vangers:

   > cmake -S . -B build-msvc -G Ninja ^
       -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=%VCPKG_ROOT%\scripts\buildsystems\vcpkg.cmake ^
       -DVCPKG_INSTALLED_DIR=%CD%\vcpkg_installed ^
       -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo ^
       -DCLUNK_ROOT=%CD%\external\clunk-install ^
       -DFFMPEG_ROOT=%CD%\external\ffmpeg
   > cmake --build build-msvc --parallel

   Note: the MSVC build currently links no executable icon. The committed
   vangers.res was produced by windres as a 32-bit COFF object, which the x64
   MSVC linker rejects (LNK1112), so MSVC builds skip the resource.

   Debug builds use the same dependencies built in Debug: vcpkg installs the
   debug packages (VCPKG_BUILD_TYPE=debug) and clunk is built with
   -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug. For FFmpeg, use the "-develop" archive, which adds
   the debug libraries under debug\ to the prefix, e.g.
   ffmpeg-9.0.2_x64-windows-shared-lgpl-develop.zip.

   The same steps are wrapped in helper scripts:

   > powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File packaging\msvc\install-msvc.ps1
   > powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File packaging\msvc\build-msvc.ps1
   > powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File packaging\msvc\run-msvc.ps1 <game-dir>

   install-msvc.ps1 installs the vcpkg dependencies, downloads a prebuilt FFmpeg
   (the "-develop" archive for Debug) and clones/builds clunk into external\.
   build-msvc.ps1 only configures and builds Vangers. Debug uses separate build
   and install trees (build-msvc-debug, external\clunk-install-debug) so it never
   mixes with the release DLLs; install and build both take -BuildType Debug.
   Visual Studio and vcpkg must be installed beforehand.
   run-msvc.ps1 copies the built game and its runtime DLLs into <game-dir> and
   launches it (add -NoRun to copy without launching). install-msvc.ps1 accepts
   -VcpkgRoot, -ClunkSrc, -ClunkRoot, -FfmpegRoot, -ClunkRepo, -ClunkCommit and
   -BuildType; build-msvc.ps1 accepts -VcpkgRoot, -ClunkRoot, -FfmpegRoot,
   -BuildDir and -BuildType; run-msvc.ps1 takes -BuildType to pick the DLLs.


OSX prerequisites

a. Install homebrew
b. brew install sdl3 sdl3_net libvorbis libogg ffmpeg toml11
c. Build/install the upstream `master` branch of clunk
   (https://github.com/stalkerg/clunk).
d. Go to 3.


Android integration

The Android host project must provide native SDL3 and SDL3_net CMake packages,
for example through their official Prefab-enabled Android archives, and an
SDL3-native Clunk build. With `ANDROID` enabled this repository builds the
static `vangers` library and exports the standard SDL3 `SDL_main()` entry point
for the host activity's native entry point. The host build must also provide a
toml11 4.4.x CMake package. Save-game files keep their existing binary format.
User preferences migrate once from `options.dat` and `controls.dat` to
`settings.toml`; the legacy files are left unchanged.
